# Hoisted node_modules layout.
#
# Why: pnpm's default `isolated` linker creates per-peer-context virtual store
# entries. When dev deps (e.g. @playwright/test → @babel/core) widen the peer
# scope of `next`, next-intl's resolution of `next` lands on a hashed path that
# differs from the app's direct `import "next/server"`. TypeScript then sees
# two structurally distinct `NextRequest` types and the build fails with
# `Property '[INTERNALS]' is missing`.
#
# `node-linker=hoisted` collapses pnpm into a flat, npm-like node_modules so
# all consumers resolve `next` to the same path. Builds are slightly larger /
# slower at install time, but the Next.js standalone output discards
# node_modules in the runner stage, so the runtime image is unaffected.
#
# Trade-off accepted: we lose pnpm's strict-peer-isolation safety net, in
# return for build determinism across docker + local + CI.
node-linker=hoisted
